in possession for a minority of the time.—Kevin Pullein\nL64: \nL65: 2\nL66: \nL67: : the group or political party having the smaller number of votes\nL68: \nL69: the Democratic/Republican minority in the Senate\nL70: \nL71: —often used before another noun\nL72: \nL73: a U.S. Senate minority leader\nL74: \nL75: 3\nL76: \nL77: a\nL78: : a part of a population thought of as differing from the rest of the population in some characteristics and often subjected to differential treatment\nL79: \nL80: the country's ethnic minorities\nL81: \nL82: minority communities\nL83: \nL84: b\nL85: \nL86: : a member of a minority group\nL87: \nL88: … Speaking as a minority, it's even more satisfying to see women have the chance to follow their dreams …—Isiah Thomas, quoted in Newsweek\nL89: \nL90: a minority student\nL91: \nL92: a minority-owned business\nL93: \nL94: —often plural\nL95: Doctors are struggling to comply with new federal guidelines requiring them to include more minorities in clinical trials …—Douglas Stanglin et al.\nL96: \nL97: 4\nL98: \nL99: a\nL100: \nL101: : the period before a person reaches the age of majority (see 【18†majority sense 2a】)\nL102: \nL103: b\nL104: \nL105: : the state of being a legal 【19†minor】\nL106: ## Synonyms of minority\nL107: \nL108: Relevance\nL109: \nL110:   * 【20†adolescence】\nL111: \nL112: 【17†See All Synonyms & Antonyms in Thesaurus】\nL113: ## Examples of minority in a Sentence\nL114: \nL115: The proposal is opposed by a minority of voters. The Republicans are now the minority in the Senate. The group is becoming an influential minority in the community.\nL116: \nL117: Recent Examples on the Web\nL118: \nL119: Examples are automatically compiled from online sources to show current usage. 【14†Read More】 Opinions expressed in the examples do not represent those of Merriam-Webster or its editors. 【21†Send us feedback†docs.google.com】.\nL120: With no side holding a clear majority, a deadlock, a minority government, a unity government – or another election – are all plausible outcomes. — Tal Shalev, CNN Money, 25 Oct. 2026 Over the past two months, amid efforts to find what could be more than $20 billion to recapitalize insurance funds, Walter has sold his controlling interest in the Lakers and a minority share of Chelsea, the English Premier League club. — Bill Shaikin, Los Angeles Times, 24 Oct.\nL121: 2026 While working for his family's auto-leasing company, Selig became a minority investor in the Milwaukee Braves, who played at County Stadium from 1953-65 and won two NL pennants and the 1957 World Series. — ABC News, 24 Oct. 2026 The National Voter Registration Act was enacted in 1993 to address barriers to voter registration that depressed participation and disproportionately affected racial minorities. — Rebecca Schneid, Time, 24 Oct. 2026 【22†See All Example Sentences for minority】\nL122: ## Word History\nL123: \nL124: First Known Use\nL125: \nL126: 15th century, in the meaning defined at 【23†sense 4a】\nL127: \nL128: Time Traveler\nL129: \nL130: The first known use of minority was in the 15th century\nL131: \nL132: 【24†See more words from the same century】\nL133: \nL134: ## Browse Nearby Words\nL135: \nL136: 【25†Minorite】\nL137: \nL138: minority\nL139: \nL140: 【26†minority leader】\nL141: \nL142: 【27†See all Nearby Words】\nL143: \nL144: ## Articles Related to minority\nL145: \nL146: 【28†Can 'majority' and 'minority' be used... It's not as simple as you may think.】\nL147: ## Cite this Entry\nL148: \nL149: Style\nL150: \nL151: [Select]\nL152: \nL153: “Minority.” Merriam-Webster.com Dictionary, Merriam-Webster, https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/minority. Accessed 1 Nov. 2026.\nL154: \nL155: 【29†Image†prod.cdn-mw.com】Copy Citation\nL156: ## Kids Definition\nL157: \nL158: minority\nL159: \nL160: noun\nL161: \nL162: mi·​nor·​i·​ty 【16†mə-ˈnȯr-ət-ē 】\nL163: \nL164: mī-,\nL165: \nL166: -ˈnär-\nL167: \nL168: plural minorities\nL169: \nL170: 1\nL171: \nL172: : the state or period of being a legal minor\nL173: \nL174: 2\nL175: \nL176: : the smaller number\nL177: \nL178: especially : a group having less than the number of votes necessary for control\nL179: \nL180: 3\nL181: \nL182: : a part of a population that differs from other groups in some characteristics and is often given unfair treatment\nL183: ## Legal Definition\nL184: \nL185: minority\nL186: \nL187: noun\nL188: \nL189: mi·​nor·​i·​ty\nL190: \nL191: plural minorities\nL192: \nL193: 1\nL194: \nL195: a\nL196: \nL197: : the period before attainment of majority\nL198: \nL199: b\nL200: \nL201: : the state of being a minor\nL202: \nL203: 2\nL204: \nL205: : the smaller of two groups constituting a whole: as\nL206: \nL207: a\nL208: \nL209: : a group (as in a legislative body) having less than the number of votes necessary for control\nL210: \nL211: b\nL212: \nL213: : a group of judges among those hearing an appeal who disagree with the majority's judgment : 【30†dissent sense 3】\nL214: \nL215: c\nL216: : a group of jurisdictions taking a less widespread approach to or view of a legal question, issue, or problem\nL217: \nL218: 3\nL219: \nL220: a\nL221: \nL222: : a part of a population differing especially from the dominant group in some characteristics (as race, sex, or national origin) and often subject to differential treatment\nL223: \nL224: b\nL225: \nL226: : a member of a minority\nL227: \nL228: an effort to hire more minorities\nL229: ## More from Merriam-Webster on minority\nL230: \nL231: Britannica.com: 【31†Encyclopedia article about minority†www.britannica.com】\nL232: \nL233: Love words? Need even more definitions?\nL234: \nL235: Subscribe to America's largest dictionary and get thousands more definitions and advanced search—ad free!\nL236: \nL237: Last Updated: 26 Oct 2026 - Updated example sentences\nL238: \nL239: ## More from Merriam-Webster\nL240: \nL241: #### 【3†Word of the Day】\nL242: \nL243: #### 【3†Pyrrhic】\nL244: \nL245: 【3†See Definitions and Examples】 »\nL246: ## Popular in Grammar & Usage\nL